All 18 point-label pairs are distinct. Labels sit below their corresponding points, with the six source Cyrillic subpart identifiers at the left. The arrows share the source's consistent left-to-right orientation, but the rows do not share a calibrated origin or unit scale.

Markers are dark circular ink marks with scan irregularities. Small light pinholes are visible in some right/middle markers, particularly rows 2, 4, and 5. There is no legend, stable filled/open distinction, or accompanying interval-endpoint convention in the figure. Filled-marker regularization must nevertheless be disclosed as a modern addition, not silently attributed to the pixels. Unequal horizontal gaps are schematic source spacing, not measured distance data.

## Verdict

PASS, independent candidate/draft figure source, mathematical, cultural, and saved-visual review only. Required revisions: none. This does not approve the full modern lesson, learner-app acceptance, publication, finalization, promotion, or durable path binding.

The source's 18 coordinate labels and six row orders are preserved. Each row still contains all three points on its own single right-directed number line. The six source subpart letters are translated sequentially to a)-f). Nothing in the candidate supplies an origin, unit tick, numerical value of x, possible/impossible verdict, or solution. Uniform accent coloring applies to every marker without classifying rows or points.

Independent judgment of the disclosed changes: straight baselines, even row spacing, typesetting, semantic colors, and uniform filled markers are acceptable modern regularization. The original pinholes are inconsistent scan/ink details in ordinary point markers, not a demonstrated open-endpoint convention. The same horizontal factor 0.68 retains the observed approximate spacing; differences between independently estimated centers and authored centers are within about two source pixels. The task explicitly asks about approximate positions. Assertion displacement values constrain drawing coordinates, not exact numerical distances among x, x^2, and x^3.

